SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Paid Family and Medical Leave Program P
 ublishes Actuarial Report\, First Claims Report
DESCRIPTION:Skip First Level Navigation\nSkip All Navigation\nMaine.gov\nA
 gencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nMaine Departmen
 t of Labor\nContact Us\n|\nNews\n|\nOnline services\n|\nPublications\n|\nS
 itemap\nHome\nContact Us\nOnline Services\nAbout MDOL\nNews & Events\nForm
 s & Publications\nMDOL Bureaus\nHome\n→\nNews & Events\n→ Maine Paid F
 amily and Medical Leave Program Publishes Actuarial Report\, First Claims 
 Report\nServices\nJobs & Training\nUnemployment Insurance\nDisability Serv
 ices\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor Laws\nLabor Statistics\nWorkforce Developmen
 t\nMaine Paid Family and Medical Leave Program Publishes Actuarial Report\
 , First Claims Report\nOctober 1\, 2026\nFor Immediate Release: Oct. 1\, 2
 026\nMedia Contact: Autumn Mahoney\, Communications Specialist media.dol@m
 aine.gov\nActuarial report projects PFML Trust Fund will remain stable for
  the next 10 years\nAUGUSTA - The Maine Paid Family and Medical Leave (PFM
 L) program has published its second annual\nactuarial report\n\, as well a
 s its first\nclaims report\non Oct. 1\, 2026.\nPFML became law in 2023 and
  is a statewide program that provides eligible workers with up to 12 weeks
  of paid leave for medical\, parental\, family care\, military family or s
 afe leave.\nThe PFML program began accepting claims on March 30\, 2026\, a
 nd began paying benefits on May 1\, 2026. This initial claims report cover
 s the program's first three months of claims activity\, from March 30 thro
 ugh June 30\, 2026. Future claims reports will be published annually and w
 ill cover a full year of claims activity.\nHighlights of the reports inclu
 de:\nThe PFML Trust Fund remains solvent and based on updated revenue and 
 claim expenditure\, is projected to remain stable for the next 10 years.\n
 The program received 8\,376 applications between March 30 and June 30\, 20
 26.\nAmong the claims approved\, medical leave was the most common reason\
 , followed by welcoming a new child and caring for a family member. Other 
 leave reasons represented a very small portion of the overall claim volume
 .\n"We are excited \nhttp://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?
 id=13363661
URL:http://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?id=13363661
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5b0571306e788379@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261001T223857Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine State Archives offers expanded digital 
 access with Open Access
DESCRIPTION:AUGUSTA\, MAINE\n– Secretary of State Shenna Bellows today a
 nnounced the official launch of Open Access\, the Maine State Archives (MS
 A) new online platform for publicly accessible\, digitized and born-digita
 l public records. The platform can be accessed at\nhttps://digitalarchives
 .maine.gov\nor from the Maine State Archives section of the Secretary of S
 tate website.\nOpen Access is the public-facing side of a strategic effort
  to maintain a digital preservation system for records of Maine state gove
 rnment. The system ensures the long-term preservation and accessibility of
  state records that are archival or subject to long-term retention.\n“Fr
 om the beginning\, I have been committed to improving efficiency and moder
 nizing our systems to benefit Mainers and streamline access\,” said\nSec
 retary Bellows\n. “Open Access will provide improved government transpar
 ency in a user-friendly platform that will be continuously expanded as mor
 e and more collections are imaged.”\nOverall\, MSA’s collections span 
 five centuries from the 1639 royal land grant to Sir Ferdinand Gorges to t
 he 2024 papers of the Lewiston Tragedy Commission. One section of the site
  is devoted to Maine history before statehood\, highlighting important rec
 ords such as Revolutionary War land grants and the Rolls and Rosters Book 
 of Orders for the 8th Division of the Maine Militia. The records of Maine
 ’s involvement in the Civil War are extensive. Open Access currently hol
 ds just over 100\,000 imaged records so far.\n“With more than 8 miles of
  documents in the Archives care\, there is a long way to go to digitize th
 em all\, but it’s a great start\,” said Maine State Archivist Christia
 n Cotz. “We’re excited to continue to add records to the platform and 
 provide Mainers from students to researchers with even more to discover ea
 ch time they visit the site.”\n###\nhttps://www.maine.gov/sos/news/maine
 -state-archives-offers-expanded-digital-access-open-access
URL:https://www.maine.gov/sos/news/maine-state-archives-offers-expanded-di
 gital-access-open-access
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
